{"qid":"Q1433867","names":["Kazakh Khanate"],"title":"Kazakh Khanate","endonym":{"native":"Қазақ хандығы","lang":"kk"},"capsule":"The Kazakh Khanate was established around 1465 by Janybek Khan and Kerei Khan, who broke away from the Uzbek confederation of Abu'l-Khayr Khan and settled on the Chu River steppe, drawing Kazakh-speaking nomads of the former Golden Horde's eastern flank. The khanate reached its apogee under Kasym Khan (r. 1509–1518), who extended control from the Syr Darya north to the Ural River and maintained diplomatic contact with Muscovy. The Dzungar invasions of the early eighteenth century — the 'Aktaban Shubryndy' catastrophe of 1723–1725 — devastated all three Kazakh Hordes (Great, Middle, and Little), pushing the Little Horde to seek Russian protection in 1731 and the Middle Horde in 1742; Russian incorporation of the Great Horde was completed by 1847.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Kazakh Khanate territorial extent is reconstructed from textual and ethnographic evidence of tribal seasonal ranges; as a nomadic steppe polity it had no fixed administrative boundaries, and the mapped extent is necessarily approximate, varying substantially by season and period.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kazakh","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Kazakh"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kazakh Khanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazakh_Khanate"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kazakh Khanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Kerei Khan","nativeName":"كرى خان","roman":"Kerei Xan","start":1465,"end":1473,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,